WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.183
Robo is a robot on a grid of squares.
It only does what it is told.

2
00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:14.744
You give it four instructions: forward,
backwards, turn left, turn right.

3
00:00:15.244 --> 00:00:18.866
Robo knows four instructions,
and only four. Here they are.

4
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:22.941
Forward moves Robo one
square the way it is facing.

5
00:00:23.161 --> 00:00:26.876
Backwards moves it one square the
other way, without turning round.

6
00:00:27.096 --> 00:00:31.508
Turn left spins Robo to its left.
It stays on the same square.

7
00:00:31.728 --> 00:00:35.582
Turn right spins it the other
way. Now Robo faces right again.

8
00:00:36.082 --> 00:00:40.122
Here is Robo on the grid. The
flower is what it must reach.

9
00:00:40.342 --> 00:00:44.290
The yellow arrow shows which way
Robo is facing. Look at it first.

10
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.132
Forward moves Robo one square
the way the arrow points.

11
00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:52.718
Turn left. The arrow spins round,
and Robo stays on its square.

12
00:00:52.938 --> 00:00:56.885
Now forward goes up the grid,
because Robo is facing a new way.

13
00:00:57.385 --> 00:01:01.982
A turn arrow spins Robo on the
spot. It moves nowhere at all.

14
00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:06.521
A forward arrow sends it one square.
Those are two different jobs.

15
00:01:06.741 --> 00:01:10.503
Count a turn as a square, and
Robo stops one square short.

16
00:01:10.723 --> 00:01:14.345
So look at the arrow every
time. Turn first, then move.

17
00:01:14.845 --> 00:01:18.793
First count the squares to the
flower. Two across, then two up.

18
00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:23.007
Now tap the arrows. The list
you build is called a program.

19
00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:28.939
Press Go. Robo runs one instruction
at a time, and reaches the flower.

20
00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.756
The order matters. Put the turn first,
and Robo ends up somewhere else.

21
00:01:33.976 --> 00:01:38.295
Robo also stops early if
it bumps into a wall. Bump!

22
00:01:38.795 --> 00:01:42.743
A good programmer knows where
Robo will stop before pressing Go.

23
00:01:42.963 --> 00:01:47.189
That is called predicting. Read
the program with your finger.

24
00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:50.706
Forward, forward. Your
finger goes up two squares.

25
00:01:50.926 --> 00:01:55.338
Turn right. The arrow points a new
way, but your finger does not move.

26
00:01:55.558 --> 00:01:59.784
One more forward, and your finger
lands on the flower. Now press Go.

27
00:02:00.284 --> 00:02:06.367
Test another program. This time Robo
stops beside the flower, not on it.

28
00:02:06.587 --> 00:02:10.581
So the program is not right
yet. Read it again, slowly.

29
00:02:10.801 --> 00:02:14.145
There is one forward too
many. Take that one away.

30
00:02:14.365 --> 00:02:19.427
Press Go again. Robo lands on the
flower. Programmers do this all day.

31
00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:24.199
Forward, backwards, turn left and
turn right. Those are the four.

32
00:02:24.419 --> 00:02:28.599
A list of them is a program, and
the order matters every time.

33
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:33.184
Predict where Robo stops. Then
press Go, and fix what went wrong.
